WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
What pulls the carbon dioxide in the air down every summer, and what pushes it back

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:14.900
up? Scientists on a Hawaiian volcano, Mauna Loa, have watched it happen every year

3
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:15.900
since 1958.

4
00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:20.010
They count carbon dioxide in parts per million.

5
00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:26.810
Four hundred parts per million means four hundred carbon dioxide molecules in every

6
00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.210
million molecules of air.

7
00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.390
So it is only a tiny part of the air.

8
00:00:32.383 --> 00:00:34.483
Here is a way to think about it.

9
00:00:35.623 --> 00:00:37.723
Picture the air as a tub that holds carbon.

10
00:00:38.923 --> 00:00:42.223
A marker on the side shows the level, in parts per million.

11
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:45.307
One process drains the tub.

12
00:00:46.527 --> 00:00:49.647
Photosynthesis takes carbon dioxide out of the air.

13
00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:54.247
The oak in a Virginia yard builds that carbon into sugar.

14
00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:57.107
Other processes fill it.

15
00:00:58.247 --> 00:01:00.807
The oak and the deer both respire, day and night.

16
00:01:02.287 --> 00:01:04.287
Decomposers in the rotting log do too.

17
00:01:05.587 --> 00:01:09.567
Burning fuel adds more, and the ocean takes some in and gives some back.

18
00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:15.847
Now the rule. The level goes up only when more flows in than flows out.

19
00:01:17.007 --> 00:01:19.827
It goes down only when more flows out than flows in.

20
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:26.790
Watch one year. In spring the oak's leaves open, and photosynthesis speeds up.

21
00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:30.770
The oak still respires, and the log still rots.

22
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.390
But out beats in, so the tub's level falls, month after month.

23
00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:40.233
In fall, photosynthesis slows and the oak drops its leaves.

24
00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:44.173
Respiration and decomposition keep going.

25
00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:49.293
Now in beats out, so the level rises through fall, winter and spring.

26
00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:52.367
Real air shows the same pattern.

27
00:01:53.607 --> 00:01:57.967
The line beside the tub is the record from Mauna Loa, for 2025.

28
00:01:59.467 --> 00:02:02.387
The station measures the air itself, not each flow.

29
00:02:03.647 --> 00:02:05.027
The highest month is May.

30
00:02:06.307 --> 00:02:08.327
After that, the level falls all summer.

31
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.900
From July to August, the monthly average dropped the most.

32
00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.740
So is August the lowest month?

33
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.040
Pause and decide.

34
00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:30.447
No. The monthly average kept falling into September, the lowest month.

35
00:02:31.687 --> 00:02:33.367
In October it was higher again.

36
00:02:34.647 --> 00:02:39.227
A tub keeps draining as long as out beats in, so the bottom comes after the biggest

37
00:02:39.227 --> 00:02:40.227
drop.

38
00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.510
Now step back to many years.

39
00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:47.070
Burning coal, oil and gas adds old carbon from underground.

40
00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.950
Each year, a little more flows in than flows out.

41
00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.530
So the whole record climbs.

42
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:58.910
To see the climb, compare the same month across the years, May with May.

43
00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.990
May against September only shows the yearly swing.

44
00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.680
One tub, two patterns.

45
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.500
The yearly swing comes from plants taking out more, then less, than is put back.

46
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.800
The long climb comes mostly from burning.
